<section id="faq" class="faq">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>FAQ</h2>
    <details><summary>Does it come with channels?</summary>
      <p>No. sbtlTV is a player. It doesn't provide, host or sell any channels, movies or shows; you use it with your own provider.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What do I need?</summary>
      <p>An Xtream login from your provider, or an M3U playlist. The guide comes from your provider, or add an XMLTV link. On Linux, install mpv from your package manager.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Which Mac does it run on?</summary>
      <p>Apple Silicon (M1 or later) with macOS 13 or later.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Windows says it protected my PC</summary>
      <p>SmartScreen can block new apps on first run. Click More info, then Run anyway.</p></details>
    <details><summary>AppImage or .deb?</summary>
      <p>The AppImage updates itself. The .deb installs like any package but doesn't update itself; download the new one from here.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Is it free?</summary>
      <p>Yes, and open source under the AGPL-3.0. The code is on <a href="https://github.com/thesubtleties/sbtlTV">GitHub</a>.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Where is my data?</summary>
      <p>On your computer. There is no sbtlTV account.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What are curated lists?</summary>
      <p>Rows on the Movies and Series home pages, like "Halloween classics", picked by hand. They only show titles your provider already has. Turn them off in Settings.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How do I report a bug?</summary>
      <p>Save a bug report in Settings > Debug and attach it to a <a href="https://github.com/thesubtleties/sbtlTV/issues">GitHub issue</a>. It leaves out logins, provider addresses and names.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Can I donate?</summary>
      <p>sbtlTV doesn't take donations. It is free and open source, and will stay that way. If you want to give something, give it to the <a href="https://supporters.eff.org/donate">Electronic Frontier Foundation</a>. They defend your right to use, study and change the software you own, and your privacy online.</p></details>
  </div>
</section>
